    "['isis network [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 point-to-point', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 point-to-point\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 point-to-point\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Cisco command \"isis network point-to-point\" is translated to Juniper\\'s \"point-to-point\" parameter under IS-IS interface configuration.\\n2. I included both interface commands since they were identical in the original Cisco config.\\n3. Juniper uses \"ge-\" prefix for GigabitEthernet interfaces instead of Cisco\\'s \"GigabitEthernet\".\\n4. The command is placed under \"protocols isis interface\" hierarchy in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 point-to-point'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 point-to-point', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 point-to-point\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 point-to-point\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Cisco command \"isis network point-to-point\" is translated to Juniper\\'s \"point-to-point\" parameter under IS-IS interface configuration.\\n2. I included both interface commands since they were identical in the original Cisco config.\\n3. Juniper uses \"ge-\" prefix for GigabitEthernet interfaces instead of Cisco\\'s \"GigabitEthernet\".\\n4. The command is placed under \"protocols isis interface\" hierarchy in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 point-to-point'}]]": 1,
    "['isis metric [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 level 2 metric 10', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `isis metric 10` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 level 2 metric 10\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 level 2 metric 10\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper:\\n1. Interfaces are named differently (ge-1/0/0 instead of GigabitEthernet1/0/0)\\n2. The metric is set per level (since IS-IS is multi-level by default)\\n3. The configuration is structured as hierarchical statements rather than interface subcommands\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 level 2 metric 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 level 2 metric 10', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `isis metric 10` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 level 2 metric 10\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 level 2 metric 10\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper:\\n1. Interfaces are named differently (ge-1/0/0 instead of GigabitEthernet1/0/0)\\n2. The metric is set per level (since IS-IS is multi-level by default)\\n3. The configuration is structured as hierarchical statements rather than interface subcommands\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 level 2 metric 10'}]]": 1,
    "['isis network [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 point-to-point', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 point-to-point\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 point-to-point\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you need to apply the point-to-point setting to each IS-IS interface separately. The equivalent configuration creates point-to-point IS-IS adjacencies on the specified interfaces, similar to Cisco\\'s \"isis network point-to-point\" command. I\\'ve included both interface translations since they both had this setting in the original config.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 point-to-point'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 point-to-point', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 point-to-point\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 point-to-point\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you need to apply the point-to-point setting to each IS-IS interface separately. The equivalent configuration creates point-to-point IS-IS adjacencies on the specified interfaces, similar to Cisco\\'s \"isis network point-to-point\" command. I\\'ve included both interface translations since they both had this setting in the original config.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 point-to-point'}]]": 1,
    "['isis metric [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 level 2 metric 10', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `isis metric 10` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 level 2 metric 10\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 level 2 metric 10\\n##\\n\\nNote:\\n1. Juniper uses a hierarchical configuration structure, so the command is split into separate statements for each interface\\n2. The metric is explicitly applied to level 2 (matching Cisco's IS-IS level-2-only configuration)\\n3. Interface naming follows Juniper's convention (ge- instead of GigabitEthernet)\\n4. In Juniper, you would need separate statements for each interface where the metric needs to be set\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 level 2 metric 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 level 2 metric 10', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `isis metric 10` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0 level 2 metric 10\\nset protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 level 2 metric 10\\n##\\n\\nNote:\\n1. Juniper uses a hierarchical configuration structure, so the command is split into separate statements for each interface\\n2. The metric is explicitly applied to level 2 (matching Cisco's IS-IS level-2-only configuration)\\n3. Interface naming follows Juniper's convention (ge- instead of GigabitEthernet)\\n4. In Juniper, you would need separate statements for each interface where the metric needs to be set\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-2/0/0 level 2 metric 10'}]]": 1,
